speechtext:
Mr. PELLETIER, moved that the House go into committee to-morrow to consider the following proposed resolutions:
Resolved, that it is expedient to amend the Civil Service Act by- _
(a)	changing the classification of the clerks in the City Post Office, offices of the Post Office Inspectors, office of Superintendent of Railway Mail Service, and in the Money Order Exchange Offices by abolishing the stampers and sorters class and fourth-class clerkships, and substituting therefor third-class clerkships, ranging from $500 to $1,000, and divided into two classes known as third-class ' A ' and ' B.'
(b)	abolishing the junior and senior second-class clerkships and substituting therefor classes ' A ' and ' B ' of the second-class, and increasing the maxinmm of the class formerly known as junior second, and now known as class ' B.' to $1,200, and increasing the maximum of class ' A,' corresponding with the former .senior second-class, to $1,400;
(c)	dividing the first-class clerkships into division ' A ' and ' B ' with maximum salaries of $1,800 and $1,600, respectively, and increasing the minimum of the first-class clerkships from $1,200 to $1,400, and creating a new class of clerks, to he known as chief clerks, in the city post offices, subject to certain restrictions;

(d)	improving the position of office superintendents in city offices, and changing what was formerly a fixed salary of $1,800 to a minimum of $1,800 and a maximum of $2,500;
(e)	providing for the employment of temporary clerks in the above named offices for a period of not more than one year, at a fixed salary of $500, and temporary helpers to meet the exigencies of the service at1 a- salary not exceeding $2.50 a day;
(f)	increasing the salaries of certain inspectors, assistant inspectors and superintendents of the Railway Mail Service in recognition of years of service;
(g)	increasing the minimum of the salaries of inspectors and superintendents of the Railway Mail Service from $1,600 to $1,800.

A special meeting was held in the council chamber last Monday evening, Mayor Cane m the chair. Present-Councillors Roadhouse, Vale, Lyons, Cane, Weslev and Dennis.
The mayor _ stated that the meeting was called to consider the question of the government stopping the work on the Holland river branch of the Trent Valley canal.
The following resolutions, moved by N J. Roadhouse, and seconded by Dr. J. H.'Wesley, was carried unanimously :-
Whereas this council has learned with regret the decision of the Minister of Railways and Canals to abandon the Holland river division of the Trent Valley canal;
Whereas this work was undertaken at the request of a large deputation representing Aurora, Newmarket, Holland Landing and Bradford, also the township of North Gwillim-bury, Whitchurch and King, which visited Ottawa and waited on the government asking that this work be undertaken for the purpose of obtaining reduced freight rates;
Whereas a large amount of freight is handled in this district, much of which would be supplied by water at a greatly reduced cost;
Whereas the route opened up by this canal was the old natural route to the north, the building of the Trent canal is again opening it up;
Whereas this work is almost completed- No. 2 section which contains the three locks and all the bridges is finished to the extent of 97
85 per cent. No. 1 section could be completed by a government dredge in a few months' time at a moderate expense;
Whereas the feeling here is strongly in favour of finishing the work;
Be it therefore resolved, that the Minister of Railways and Canals be requested to withhold his decision until further investigation has been made;
That copies of this resolution be sent to the right lion. Prime Minister, the hon. Minister of Railways and Canals, and the member for North York.
Several citizens spoke in favour of tlie resolution before the vote was taken.
Council adjourned.
I desire to ask the Prime Minister (Mr. Borden) or the Minister of Railways (Mr. Cochrane) whether this matter has been brought to their notice in the form stated in this report.
